@lesson Session 2 · Greetings, jobs & questions
Hallo = hello
Tschüs = bye
Mach es gut! / Mach's gut! = Take care! / All the best!
Guten Morgen = good morning
Guten Tag = good day / hello
Guten Abend = good evening
Gute Nacht = good night
Auf Wiedersehen = goodbye (formal)
Wie geht's? / Wie geht es dir? = How are you? (informal)
Wie geht es Ihnen? = How are you? (formal)
Super! / Sehr gut! = Super! / Very good! | Wie geht's dir? {{Super}}. Und dir? Auch super. | How are you? Super. And you? Also super.
Gut. / Gut, danke. = Good. / Good, thanks.
Es geht. = It's okay. / So-so. | Wie geht's dir? {{Es geht}}. Und dir? Gut, danke. | How are you? So-so. And you? Good, thanks.
Nicht so gut. = Not so good.
Und dir? = And you? (informal) | | | Used in the mini-dialogues
der Ingenieur = engineer (m.)
die Ingenieurin = engineer (f.)
der Arzt = doctor (m.) | | | Feminine: die Ärztin (adds -in and an umlaut)
die Ärztin = doctor (f.)
der Verkäufer = salesperson / shop assistant (m.)
die Verkäuferin = salesperson / shop assistant (f.)
der Kellner = waiter
die Kellnerin = waitress
der Student = student (m.)
die Studentin = student (f.)
der Journalist = journalist (m.)
die Journalistin = journalist (f.)
der Architekt = architect (m.)
die Architektin = architect (f.)
der Lehrer = teacher (m.)
die Lehrerin = teacher (f.)
der Friseur = hairdresser (m.)
die Friseurin = hairdresser (f.)
der Kfz-Mechatroniker = automotive mechatronics technician (m.)
die Kfz-Mechatronikerin = automotive mechatronics technician (f.)
der Paketzusteller = delivery driver / courier (m.) | Ich bin {{Paketzusteller}} und arbeite bei HotSped. | I am a delivery driver and work at HotSped.
die Paketzustellerin = delivery driver / courier (f.)
Was machst du beruflich? = What do you do for a living? (informal)
Was bist du von Beruf? = What is your profession? (informal)
was = what | {{Was}} ist das? | What is that?
wer = who | {{Wer}} ist das? | Who is that?
wann = when | {{Wann}} kommst du? | When are you coming?
wo = where | {{Wo}} wohnst du? | Where do you live?
woher = where from | {{Woher}} kommst du? | Where are you from?
wohin = where to | {{Wohin}} gehst du? | Where are you going?
warum = why | {{Warum}} lernst du Deutsch? | Why are you learning German?
wie = how | {{Wie}} geht es dir? | How are you?
welcher / welche / welches = which | {{Welche}} Sprache sprichst du? | Which language do you speak?
wie viel = how much | {{Wie viel}} kostet das? | How much does that cost?
wie viele = how many | {{Wie viele}} Bücher hast du? | How many books do you have?
das Buch (Pl. -"er) = book | Wie viele {{Bücher}} hast du? | How many books do you have? | Plural from the W-Fragen example
kommen = to come | Woher {{kommst}} du? | Where are you from?
wohnen = to live (reside) | Wo {{wohnst}} du? | Where do you live?
lernen = to learn | Warum {{lernst}} du Deutsch? | Why are you learning German?
gehen = to go | Wohin {{gehst}} du? | Where are you going?
leben = to live | Ich {{lebe}} allein. | I live alone.
sagen = to say | | | Page 11: sagen → sagt
Ich komme aus ... = I come from ... | Ich komme aus {{Deutschland}}. | I come from Germany.
Ich komme aus dem Iran. = I come from Iran. | | | The country Iran takes the article "dem"
@grammar W-Fragen
! W-Fragen all start with W. The conjugated verb is always in position 2: W-word + verb + subject / rest?
@grammar Job titles
! Feminine job titles usually add -in to the masculine one, and sometimes an umlaut: der Arzt → die Ärztin.
@grammar Regular verbs
! Regular verbs: take the stem and add -e · -st · -t · -en · -t · -en (ich · du · er/sie/es · wir · ihr · sie/Sie).
Ich {{komme}} aus Deutschland. | I come from Germany. | ich → -e
Woher {{kommst}} du? | Where are you from? | du → -st
Woher {{kommen}} Sie? | Where are you from? (formal) | Sie → -en
Wo {{wohnst}} du? | Where do you live? | du → -st
Wohin {{gehst}} du? | Where are you going? | du → -st
Warum {{lernst}} du Deutsch? | Why are you learning German? | du → -st
Ich {{lebe}} allein. | I live alone. | ich → -e
Ja, wir {{leben}} zusammen. | Yes, we live together. | wir → -en
{{Lebt}} ihr zusammen? | Do you live together? | ihr → -t
Was {{machst}} du beruflich? | What do you do for a living? | du → -st
@grammar aus + country
! Ich komme aus ... (the country). For Iran you need the article: Ich komme aus dem Iran.
Ich komme aus {{dem}} Iran. | I come from Iran. | Iran needs the article "dem"
